  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1006 PM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nSouthwesterly flow ahead of a deep trough along the west coast \nis set up overhead. Amidst this flow, a pool of substantial \nmoisture is nudging into southern Utah per water vapor imagery. \nAccording to ensembles, we should expect this pool to continue \nseeping into the Intermountain West over the coming 48 hours, \nbringing impacts to our CWA. Per the 00Z GJT sounding, low-\nlevels are still very dry, and model guidance suggests that even\nas we see the plume of moisture begin drifting from the west \nSunday, it will take some time for near- surface conditions to \nsaturate enough to support significant rainfall. With this, look\nfor Sunday's storm coverage to be generally isolated for most \nof the region aside from northeastern Utah where the stream of \nmoisture will be the strongest.\n\nOvernight Sunday into Monday morning, the moisture plume \ncenters itself over the CWA. With stabilization from nighttime \ncooling and without any significant upper-level dynamics or \nforcing mechanisms, look for precipitation to be steady and \nrelatively light throughout the night. Where things get more \ninteresting is after sunrise on Monday. Though the bulk of the \nmoisture will have come and gone, wetter low-levels will remain,\nsupporting more storm development. The caveat to these storms \nis the denser cloud cover that is anticipated to be in place \nduring the morning hours, dampening destabilization. What this \nmeans for us is that storm mode is trending in a more stratiform\ndirection instead of discrete cells capable of heavier \nlocalized rainfall. Some early CAM guidance suggests that a few \npockets of 300+ J/kg of CAPE may manifest in spite of the \nmorning cloud cover, but this likely would result in little more\nthan a few stronger cells embedded within stratiform rainfall. \nThough this supports a broader areal coverage of wetting rains \nwithout amplifying the hydrologic threat, we have seen in \nrecent days that even stratiform rains are capable of producing \nflash flooding, partially due to the more saturated nature of \nsoils region-wide. So don't go discounting the flash flood \nthreat come Monday even should stratiform storms dominate. This \nis especially true for vulnerable areas and recent burn scars.\n\nBeyond Monday, the trough to our west breaks down and transits \neastwards, producing more zonal flow across much of the CONUS. \nThis elongates the Southern Plains high, pushing the high \nwestwards towards us and re-establishing in the southwestern \nCONUS. Exactly how far west the high will get shoved is a point \nof uncertainty, one which potentially holds significant \nramifications on mid-to-late-week storm potential. Moisture \nfrom the Pacific is expected to get drawn around the high into \nthe southwestern US. Regardless of the high's position, we are \nunlikely to be in a favorable position to receive the brunt of \nthis moisture, but the exact trajectory of the pool will still \nhave notable impacts on the resulting precipitation over the \nWest Slope. At the very least, daily showers along the high \nterrain are expected throughout the week. As it stands, \nuncertainty is too high to speculate on the details, so stay \ntuned over the coming days for additional information.",
